{
  "id": "a1-04",
  "kind": "lesson",
  "level": "A1",
  "order": 5,
  "title": "The Genitive",
  "summary": "The case of *of*: possession, origin, quantity, and the object of a negated verb. It is the commonest case in Lithuanian and the one an A1 learner meets in the most different jobs.",
  "rules": [
    {
      "id": "genitive-endings",
      "number": "28",
      "heading": "The Genitive Singular",
      "statement": "The **genitive** answers *kieno?* (whose?) and *ko?* (of what?). Its singular endings are given below. Note that masculine **-is** and **-ys** fall together in **-io**, exactly as they fell together in the accusative (¶20), and that the genitive is the case in which a noun is cited second in every dictionary — it is what tells you which declension the word belongs to.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "Nominative → genitive singular",
          "columns": [
            "nominative",
            "genitive",
            "example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "masc. -as",
              "cells": [
                "-as",
                "-o",
                "namas → namo"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "masc. -is",
              "cells": [
                "-is",
                "-io",
                "brolis → brolio"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "masc. -ys",
              "cells": [
                "-ys",
                "-io",
                "obuolys → obuolio"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "masc. -us",
              "cells": [
                "-us",
                "-aus",
                "sūnus → sūnaus"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "fem. -a",
              "cells": [
                "-a",
                "-os",
                "knyga → knygos"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "fem. -ė",
              "cells": [
                "-ė",
                "-ės",
                "katė → katės"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "footnote": "The feminines in -is decline apart: moteris → moters, naktis → nakties."
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "brolio knyga",
          "gloss": "the brother's book"
        },
        {
          "target": "namo langas",
          "gloss": "the window of the house"
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "accusative-nouns",
        "noun-endings"
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "genitive-possession",
      "number": "29",
      "heading": "The Genitive of Possession",
      "statement": "The possessor stands in the genitive and **precedes** the thing possessed, as in English \"the brother's book\" and never as in \"the book of the brother\". Lithuanian has no word answering to *of*: the ending does the whole work.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "mokytojo namas",
          "gloss": "the teacher's house"
        },
        {
          "target": "Rūtos katė",
          "gloss": "Rūta's cat",
          "note": "proper names decline like any other noun"
        },
        {
          "target": "mergaitės knyga",
          "gloss": "the girl's book"
        }
      ],
      "footnotes": [
        "Reversing the order reverses the sense: *mokytojo namas* is the teacher's house, *namo mokytojas* the house's teacher."
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "genitive-endings"
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "genitive-origin",
      "number": "30",
      "heading": "The Genitive of Origin",
      "statement": "**Iš** (from, out of) governs the genitive, and with it a speaker says where they come from — one of the first things asked of a foreigner in Lithuania. The question is *Iš kur tu esi?*",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Aš esu iš Lietuvos.",
          "gloss": "I am from Lithuania.",
          "note": "Lietuva → Lietuvos"
        },
        {
          "target": "Ji yra iš Vilniaus.",
          "gloss": "She is from Vilnius.",
          "note": "Vilnius → Vilniaus, a -us noun"
        },
        {
          "target": "Iš kur tu esi?",
          "gloss": "Where are you from?"
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "genitive-endings",
        "buti-present"
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "genitive-negation",
      "number": "31",
      "heading": "The Genitive after a Negated Verb",
      "statement": "The rule stated for recognition in ¶26 may now be given its reason. A negated verb asserts that **no part** of the thing was affected, and the genitive is the case of the part rather than of the whole; so the direct object of a negated transitive verb leaves the accusative and takes the genitive. This is not optional and admits no exception.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Aš matau namą. → Aš nematau namo.",
          "gloss": "I see a house. → I do not see a house."
        },
        {
          "target": "Ji turi knygą. → Ji neturi knygos.",
          "gloss": "She has a book. → She has no book."
        },
        {
          "target": "Mes valgome duoną. → Mes nevalgome duonos.",
          "gloss": "We eat bread. → We do not eat bread."
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "negation-genitive",
        "genitive-endings",
        "accusative-nouns"
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "genitive-quantity",
      "number": "32",
      "heading": "The Genitive of Quantity",
      "statement": "A word of measure or quantity is followed by the **genitive** of the thing measured — the partitive genitive, so called because it names the whole out of which a part is taken. This is how prices are asked and shopping done, and it governs *kiek?* (how much, how many) as well.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "kilogramas obuolių",
          "gloss": "a kilogram of apples",
          "note": "obuoliai → obuolių, genitive plural"
        },
        {
          "target": "puodelis kavos",
          "gloss": "a cup of coffee"
        },
        {
          "target": "daug vandens",
          "gloss": "much water"
        },
        {
          "target": "Kiek duonos?",
          "gloss": "How much bread?"
        }
      ],
      "footnotes": [
        "The genitive plural ends in -ų for every declension: namų, brolių, knygų, kačių. It is taken up in full with the plural."
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "genitive-endings"
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "genitive-prepositions",
      "number": "33",
      "heading": "Prepositions Governing the Genitive",
      "statement": "Most Lithuanian prepositions govern one case and one only, and the commonest govern the **genitive**: *iš* (from), *nuo* (from, off), *iki* (until, as far as), *prie* (near, at), *ant* (on), *be* (without), *po* (after, of time). Learning a preposition means learning its case with it; a preposition with the wrong case is as wrong as a wrong preposition.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "Prepositions with the genitive",
          "columns": [
            "preposition",
            "meaning",
            "example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "iš",
              "cells": [
                "from, out of",
                "iš namo — out of the house"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "nuo",
              "cells": [
                "from, off",
                "nuo stalo — off the table"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "iki",
              "cells": [
                "until, as far as",
                "iki vakaro — until evening"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "prie",
              "cells": [
                "near, at",
                "prie lango — by the window"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "ant",
              "cells": [
                "on",
                "ant stalo — on the table"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "be",
              "cells": [
                "without",
                "be duonos — without bread"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "po",
              "cells": [
                "after (of time)",
                "po pietų — after lunch"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "footnote": "Note that *ant*, on, takes the genitive where English feeling would expect a place-case; and that *po* with the instrumental means \"under\" instead, which is taken up with that case."
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Knyga guli ant stalo.",
          "gloss": "The book is lying on the table."
        },
        {
          "target": "Kava be pieno.",
          "gloss": "Coffee without milk."
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "genitive-endings"
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "genitive-questions",
      "number": "34",
      "heading": "Asking with the Genitive",
      "statement": "The interrogative *kas* has its own genitive **ko** (of what), and for persons **kieno** (whose). A question is answered in the case the question word stands in — which is why knowing the case of a question word is knowing the shape of its answer.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Kieno tai knyga? — Brolio.",
          "gloss": "Whose book is this? — The brother's."
        },
        {
          "target": "Ko tu neturi? — Pinigų.",
          "gloss": "What do you not have? — Money."
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "genitive-possession",
        "genitive-negation"
      ],
      "core": false
    }
  ],
